<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giacomo Puccini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giacomo Puccini was an Italian composer, the leading figure of Italian opera after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Giuseppe Verdi</a>. His works, including La bohème (1896), Tosca (1900), Madama Butterfly (1904) and the unfinished Turandot, combine lyrical melody with realistic drama and remain among the most performed operas in the world. He was born in Lucca and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giacomo-puccini.jpg" alt="Giacomo Puccini" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miscellaneous Items in High Demand, PPOC, Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giacomo_Puccini_LCCN2005685154_(1)_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
